A block-level element (not in the inline set, so not an atom) glued into
prose by a significant space keeps its segment together — that segment's own
Fill still wraps the surrounding prose at word gaps. An edge-unsafe list
(leading or trailing significant space) stays fully inline exactly as before,
even with a block child in it — the edge guard, not atom status, decides. A
`<pre>` subtree containing an inline-tag element must stay verbatim: preserve
tags disqualify every element beneath them from atom status, since preserved
text may hold significant newlines an atom's flat rendering would eat.

-- input.gsx --
package p

component BlockGluedIntoProse() {
	<div>
		Some long lead-in prose that talks about the widget below and keeps going for a while before the block starts <div class="callout">Note: read the docs.</div> and then some trailing prose continues right after the block, glued to it by a literal space so it has to share the block's segment.
	</div>
}

component EdgeUnsafeListStaysFlat() {
	<p> lead <div>inside</div> trail </p>
}

component PreWithInlineChild() {
	<pre><code>fn <em>highlight</em> stays verbatim here, never folded into an atom's flat line</code></pre>
}
